powerpc/powernv: restore pnv_npu2_unmap_lpar_dev()

Patch 0001 restored pnv_npu2_map_lpar_dev() but not its inverse.  As a
result the NPU translation context armed at boot (and re-armed on every
release-ownership transition once patch 0004 lands) is never destroyed:
opal_npu_init_context() has no matching opal_npu_destroy_context(), and the
GPU's BDF is never mapped back to LPAR 0.  On any teardown path -- VFIO
taking ownership of an NVLink GPU, an NPU PE being released, an unbind --
that leaks an XTS context in firmware.

Restore the function from v5.12 arch/powerpc/platforms/powernv/npu-dma.c
lines 669-705, plus declarations in <asm/pci.h> (where v5.12 declared it,
line 127) and in the powernv-private pci.h.

API adaptations vs v5.12
------------------------
1. NVLink1 detection.  v5.12 tested "hose->npu == NULL".  struct
   pci_controller lost its npu member when the nvlink code was deleted in
   v5.13 and this series does not re-add it (patch 0004 keeps the NPU
   descriptor in the powernv-private struct pnv_phb instead).  The test is
   therefore done on the PHB device-tree node's compatible string,
   "ibm,ioda2-npu2-phb", exactly as patch 0001's pnv_npu2_map_lpar_dev()
   already does.  Identical outcome on POWER9/NPU2.

2. No other change.  pci_dev_id(), dev_info_once(), of_device_is_compatible()
   and opal_npu_destroy_context()/opal_npu_map_lpar() all have unchanged
   signatures in 6.12.

Risk: LOW.  The function has no in-tree caller until patch 0004; it is dead
code plus an exported symbol on its own.  It cannot change boot behaviour.

